        {
            "id": "32800441",
            "user": "Bhoimus",
            "timestamp": "07:33, 23 September 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,961 bytes)",
            "change": -14,
            "summary": "(RCP reverted edits by 217.165.138.71 (talk) to  revision #32077287 by Votebot)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "When connecting electrical devices to a power source, they can be hooked up to make either a series circuit or a parallel circuit. In a parallel circuit, the electrical current flows along several paths, and each individual device is hooked up to its own circuit. The advantage to a parallel circuit is that if one device malfunctions, the flow of electricity will not stop, as it will in a series circuit. Additionally, several devices can be hooked up to the power source at once, without decreasing the overall wattage output. Creating your own parallel circuit is simple, and is thus a great project to perform to be able to see for yourself how electricity works."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "When connecting electrical devices to a power source, they can be hooked up to make either a series circuit or a parallel circuit. In a parallel circuit, the electrical current flows along several paths, and each individual device is hooked up to its own circuit. The advantage to a parallel circuit is that if one device malfunctions, the flow of electricity will not stop, as it will in a series circuit. Additionally, several devices can be hooked up to the power source at once, without decreasing the overall wattage output. Creating your own parallel circuit is simple, and '''oke,oke",
                    "'''is thus a great project to perform to be able to see for yourself how electricity works."
                ]
            }
        },
