        {
            "id": "24410026",
            "user": "WikiHow Expert Review",
            "timestamp": "19:44, 19 April 2018",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,094 bytes)",
            "change": 66,
            "summary": "(Incorporating expert feedback)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "Gabriella Calderon",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Choose your load. This is the item that you'll be hooking up to the power source. You can make a parallel circuit with light bulbs (you'll need 2); flashlight bulbs are also a good choice.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare your conductors. Use aluminum foil as your conductor to build this type of parallel circuit. The foil will be used to connect the power source to the loads.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Cut the foil into 4 narrow strips: 2 {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} pieces, and 2 {{convert|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} pieces. They should be narrow, about the width of a drinking straw.",
                    "#*Take one of the {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} strips of foil and connect it to the positive terminal of the battery. <ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Take the other {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} strip and connect it to the negative terminal of the battery. <ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Take the 2 shorter {{convert|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} strips and wrap one end of each around the long strip coming off the positive terminal. Place one {{convert|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} strip near the top of the strip, and the other about {{convert|3|in|cm}} down toward the battery.<ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Wrap the loose ends of the shorter strips around your 2 light bulbs. You may find it helpful to secure the strips with electrical tape.",
                    "#*Place the ends of the 2 light bulbs against the {{convert|8|in|cm|abbr=on}} strip of foil that is attached to the negative terminal of the battery.",
                    "#Choose this method for a slightly advanced project. While creating a parallel circuit is not complicated, this method requires you to use wire and a switch; it may be suited for slightly older students.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Gather the main components of a parallel circuit. You don't need much to complete this project: You need a power source, a conducting material, at least 2 loads (the items which use electricity), and a switch.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Use a 9-volt battery as the power source.",
                    "#*You'll be cutting the wire into several pieces, so make sure you have plenty ({{convert|30-40|in|cm}} should do it).",
                    "#*For the load, use light bulbs or flashlight bulbs.",
                    "#*Cut the wire into five pieces (between {{convert|6-8|in|cm}} will be fine).",
                    "#*Carefully remove approximately {{convert|1/2|in|cm}} of the insulation from both ends of all your wire pieces.",
                    "#Connect the first lightbulb to the battery. Attach 1 of the wires to the positive terminal of the battery and wind the other end around the left side of 1 of the lightbulbs. [[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Complete the parallel circuit. Using your remaining piece of wire, wrap 1 end around the right side of the first lightbulb, and the other end around the right side of the second lightbulb.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "#Choose your load. This is the item that you'll be hooking up to the power source. We'll describe making a parallel circuit with lightbulbs (you'll need two); flashlight bulbs are also a good choice.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare your conductors. You are using aluminum foil as your conductor in this method of building a parallel circuit. The foil will be used to connect the power source to the loads.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* Cut the foil into four narrow strips: two 8\" pieces, and two 4\" pieces. They should be narrow, about the width of a drinking straw.",
                    "#*Take one of the 8\" strips of foil and connect it to the positive terminal of the battery. <ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Take the other 8\" strip and connect it to the negative terminal of the battery. <ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Take the two shorter, 4\"  strips and wrap one end of each around the long strip coming off the positive terminal. (Place one 4\"  strip near the top of the strip, and the other about three inches down towards the battery.)<ref>http://www.education.com/science-fair/article/parallel/</ref>",
                    "#*Wrap the loose ends of the shorter strips around your two lightbulbs. You may find it helpful to secure the strips with electrical tape.",
                    "#*Place the ends of the two lightbulbs against the 8\" strip of foil that is attached to the negative terminal of the battery.",
                    "",
                    "#Choose this method as a slightly advanced project. While creating a parallel circuit is not complicated, this method requires you to use wire and a switch; it may be suited for slightly older students.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Gather the main components of a parallel circuit. You don't need much to complete this project: You need a power source, a conducting material, at least two loads (the items which use electricity), and a switch.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Our directions call for you to use a battery as your power source. A 9-volt battery will work well.",
                    "#*You'll be cutting the wire into several pieces, so make sure you have plenty (30-40\" should do it).",
                    "#*Our plans use lightbulbs as the load of choice, but flashlight bulbs are also a good choice.",
                    "#*Cut the wire into five pieces (between 6\" and 8\" will be fine).",
                    "#*Carefully remove approximately ½\" of the insulation from both ends of all your wire pieces.",
                    "#Connect the first lightbulb to the battery. Attach one of the wires to the positive terminal of the battery and wind the other end around the left side of one of the lightbulbs. [[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Complete the parallel circuit. Using your remaining piece of wire, wrap one end around the right side of the first lightbulb, and the other end around the right side of the second lightbulb.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
