        {
            "id": "20621427",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "12:36, 20 July 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(7,990 bytes)",
            "change": 130,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Consider the age and skill of those involved. Creating a parallel circuit is an excellent and easy experiment for students learning about electricity. This method of building a parallel circuit is excellent for younger students: they may have limited dexterity, and you may not want them to use sharp tools.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Choose your power source. The cheapest and most convenient power source for your parallel circuit project is a battery. A 9-volt battery is an excellent choice.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Choose your load. This is the item that you'll be hooking up to the power source. We'll describe making a parallel circuit with lightbulbs (you'll need two); flashlight bulbs are also a good choice.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare your conductors. You are using aluminum foil as your conductor in this method of building a parallel circuit. The foil will be used to connect the power source to the loads.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the first of the conducting strips to the battery. You're now ready to start hooking up your parallel circuit.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Hook up your lightbulbs. You're now ready to connect your loads to the conducting material.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Complete the parallel circuit. Once you finish connecting all of the elements of the parallel circuit, your lightbulbs should glow.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Choose this method as a slightly advanced project. While creating a parallel circuit is not complicated, this method requires you to use wire and a switch; it may be suited for slightly older students.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Gather the main components of a parallel circuit. You don't need much to complete this project: You need a power source, a conducting material, at least two loads (the items which use electricity), and a switch.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Prepare your wires. The wire is your conducting material, which will create the circuits between the power source and your leads.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the first lightbulb to the battery. Attach one of the wires to the positive terminal of the battery and wind the other end around the left side of one of the lightbulbs. <ref>http://www.sciencefairadventure.com/Make_a_Parallel_Circuit.aspx</ref>[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 11 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Begin to connect the switch to the battery. Take a separate piece of wire and connect it to the negative terminal of the battery. Take the other end of the wire and connect it to the switch. <ref>http://www.sciencefairadventure.com/Make_a_Parallel_Circuit.aspx</ref>[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the switch to the first lightbulb. Using yet another piece of wire, connect it first to the switch, and then wind it around the right side of the first lightbulb. <ref>http://www.sciencefairadventure.com/Make_a_Parallel_Circuit.aspx</ref>[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Connect the second lightbulb. Take your fourth piece of wire and wind it around the left side of the first lightbulb, and then wind the other end around the left side of the second lightbulb. <ref>http://www.sciencefairadventure.com/Make_a_Parallel_Circuit.aspx</ref>[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Complete the parallel circuit. Using your remaining piece of wire, wrap one end around the right side of the first lightbulb, and the other end around the right side of the second lightbulb.[[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 15 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn on the switch. Flip the switch, and you should see both bulbs light up. Congratulations—you successfully built a parallel circuit![[Image:Make a Parallel Circuit Step 16 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
